1314 0 obj
<<
/Author (Gerald G\056 Marten\054 Xenia Caballero\054 Arnulfo Larios and Hilda Benda\361a)
/CreationDate (D\07220220330150145\05510\04700\047)
/Creator (Adobe Acrobat 9\0560\0560)
/ModDate (D\07220261015163908Z)
/Producer (Adobe Acrobat 9\0560\0560)
/Subject (Monte Verde\054 a peri\055urban squatter community near San Pedro Sula\054 virtually eliminated Aedes aegypti production in all known larval habitats\072 wells\073 water storage containers including pilas \050open concrete water tanks used for laundry\051\054 200\055liter drums\054 1000\055liter plastic \215cisterns\054\216 buckets\073 and objects collecting rainwater\056 The project began in 2016 when Monte Verde was overrun with dengue\054 Zika\054 and chikungunya\056 During more than a year of experimentation\054 Monte Verde residents crafted an effective\054 sustainable\054 and environmentally friendly toolkit that was inexpensive but required full community participation\056 Biological control with copepods\054 turtles\054 and tilapia was at the core of the toolkit\054 along with a mix of other methods such as getting rid of unnecessary containers\054 scrubbing them to remove Ae\056 aegypti eggs\054 and covering them to exclude mosquitoes or rainwater\056 Environmentally friendly larvicides also had a limited but crucial role\056 Key design features\072 \0501\051 toolkit components known to be nearly 100\045 effective at preventing Ae\056 aegypti production when fitted to appropriate larval habitats\073 \0502\051 using Ae\056 aegypti larval habitats as a resource by transforming them into \215egg sinks\216 to drive Ae\056 aegypti population decline\073 \0503\051 dedicated community volunteers who worked with their neighbors\054 targeting 100\045 coverage of all known Ae\056 aegypti larval habitats with an appropriate control method\073 \0504\051 monthly monitoring in which the volunteers visited every house to assess progress and improve coverage as an ongoing learning experience for both volunteers and residents\056 Taking pupae as an indicator of Ae\056 aegypti production\054 from September 2018 to the end of the record in December 2021 \050except for a brief lapse during COVID lockdown in 2020\051\054 the monthly count of pupae fluctuated between zero and 0\0566\045 of the 22\054984 pupae counted in the baseline survey at the beginning of the project\056 Adult Ae\056 aegypti declined to low numbers but did not disappear completely\056 There were no recognizable cases of dengue\054 Zika\054 or chikungunya after June 2018\054 though the study design based on a single site did not provide a basis for rigorous confirmation that Monte Verde\220s Ae\056 aegypti control program was responsible\056 Nonetheless\054 Monte Verde\220s success at eliminating Ae\056 aegypti production can serve as a model for extending this approach to other communities\056 Key ingredients for success were outside stimulation and facilitation to foster shared community awareness and commitment regarding the problem and its solution\054 enduring commitment of local leadership\054 compatibility of the toolkit with the local community\054 overcoming social obstacles\054 rapid results with \215success breeding success\054\216 and building resilience\056)
/Title (Proof of concept for eliminating Aedes aegypti production by means of integrated control including turtles\054 copepods\054 tilapia\054 larvicides\054 and community participation in Monte Verde\054 Honduras)
/Keywords (Aedes aegypti\073 integrated control\073 biological control\073 community participation\073 Monte Verde\073 dengue prevention)
>>
